<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uriah Butler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tubal Uriah Butler</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tubal Uriah 'Buzz' Butler was a Grenadian-born labour leader and preacher in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>. A former oilfield worker, he led the oilfield strike of June 1937, which spread into unrest across Trinidad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a> and led to the recognition of trade unions and labour reforms. Interned during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, he later led his own political party. He is a National Hero of Trinidad and Tobago.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uriah-butler.jpg" alt="Uriah Butler" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grueslayer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TnT_Fyzabad_Uriah_Butler_Statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
